/-! # Campaign 31 (`r7443+71`): row 62's claim 1, the `ε⁴` scalar of `exp(εH)` on `S³` as a universal polynomial in the frame jet

The receipt (`P10_the_covariant_quartic_is_seven_rationals_…py`, claim 1) works in the left-invariant
orthonormal frame on `S³`. There the metric is `δ` and `C^c_ab = 2 ε_abc`. So the `ε⁴` coefficient of `R[exp(εH)]`
is "a universal polynomial in `(H, eH, eeH)` with rational coefficients, an identity in the jet".

This is campaign 29's frame engine with `H` a FREE jet.
- `h : Fin 5 → K` are the coordinates of the symmetric traceless `H`, and `d a` those of `D_a H`.
- `D_a D_c H` is `s (ac)` plus the antisymmetric part the frame commutator fixes, `[D_a, D_c] = 2 ε_acm D_m`
  (`DDt`).
- `jet_series`: the scalar series' coefficients `0..4` are `scT0..scT4`, explicit polynomials in the fifty jet
  coordinates. Every layer table was generated by sympy and each one is proved from the layer below it.
- `frame_constant_closed_form`: at `d = s = 0` this is `6 − 2p₂ε² − (10/3)p₃ε³ − (7/12)p₂²ε⁴` for EVERY symmetric
  traceless `H`, diagonal or not.

The frame Koszul formula is the definition of the scalar curvature, as in campaigns 24 and 29. -/

